From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 11:24:25 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0383 -- remove patch 0382's TEMPORARY switch
 DOSAGS_ROUTE_FAST and the old search bodies: the legacy route finder's stamp
 grid, frontier walk and inline line-of-sight walk are now the only code
 (operator 2026-11-17 after round RR: "On, remove old code")

WHY: 0382 put the three output-identical levers behind a temporary, default-off switch for a same-binary 486 A/B and
stated its own removal. Round RR (Gateway 486DX2-66, MS3, ABBA of the switch; tests/harness/results/ROUNDRR-2026-11-17)
read R-SKIPS SUPPORTED: skipped frames 578 -> 364 per run (O 558 / 572 / 605, F 359 / 324 / 409), displayed 91.7% ->
94.8%, all six cells valid, the stamp grid at 192,000 bytes at most. The operator ruled the fast search the default and
the old code out.

WHAT:
- the switch is gone: dos_rf_fast / dos_rf_fast_on() and both tests of it (in __find_route and in can_see_from);
- the bodies that lost are gone: the old find_route_dijkstra and its CHECK_MIN. find_route_dijkstra_fast and
  CHECK_MIN_FAST take their names and their PLACE in the file, so against the pre-0382 text the function is edited in
  place: CHECK_MIN's first test reads the stamp, the 0xFF fill is the stamp's begin, the two writes stamp, the pass loop
  walks the active-slot bitset, three writes keep the bitset;
- can_see_from: an 8-bit mask takes the inline walk; other depths keep do_line and line_callback;
- DOSAGS_ROUTE_FAST leaves the environment witness list (quit.cpp is back to its pre-0382 text);
- the exit line "DOS-PORT routefast on= stamp_allocs= stamp_peak_bytes=" becomes "DOS-PORT routestamp allocs=
  peak_bytes=": the switch witness goes with the switch, the memory witness stays (two counters that move only when a
  mask size changes).
- Unchanged: the stamp grid (W x H bytes, one at a time, allocated at the first search on a mask size, freed and
  re-allocated on a size change, freed in ~LegacyRouteFinder; quit on a failed allocation), the wrap at generation 255.
